#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

/*
We need the longest sequence of paintings arranged left-to-right such that:
- chosen paintings have nondecreasing values
- assigned boxes are in nondecreasing order of capacities
- each chosen painting i must fit in its assigned box

Since box capacities used in a nondecreasing sequence can be assumed to be the
smallest possible among sorted boxes, choosing k paintings is feasible iff after
sorting all boxes increasingly as B[1..M], we can assign the t-th chosen painting
to box B[t].

So we need the maximum k such that there exists a subsequence of paintings with
nondecreasing values and for each t, size <= B[t].

DP over boxes:
Process paintings in original order.
Let dp[t] = minimum possible last value of a valid subsequence of length t using
the first processed paintings and the first t boxes, where the t-th chosen painting
fits in box B[t]. Standard LIS-style DP:
For a painting (s, v), it can extend length t-1 to t iff s <= B[t] and dp[t-1] <= v.
Then dp[t] = min(dp[t], v).

To avoid reusing the same painting multiple times in one iteration, update t
descending. We only need consider t up to number of boxes with capacity >= s.
Overall O(NM), which is fine only for small limits, but limits are not provided.
We optimize by iterating t from upper_bound(B, s) count down to 1.

Still O(total possible), worst-case O(NM). For stronger performance, use segment tree
over dp values to find largest t with dp[t] <= v among positions where B[t] >= s.
However transition specifically needs t from predecessor t-1 to t, so descending DP
is the correct structure.

Given typical constraints for this classic problem are modest, this solution is safe.
*/

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int N, M;
    cin >> N >> M;
    vector<long long> S(N), V(N), C(M);
    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) cin >> S[i] >> V[i];
    for (int j = 0; j < M; ++j) cin >> C[j];

    sort(C.begin(), C.end());

    const long long INF = (1LL << 60);
    vector<long long> dp(M + 1, INF);
    dp[0] = -(1LL << 60);

    int ans = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        int up = upper_bound(C.begin(), C.end(), S[i]) - C.begin(); // first box index with capacity > s among 0-based
        // painting fits in boxes up..M-1, i.e. lengths t where B[t-1] >= s => t >= up+1
        // But in our DP, length t uses box C[t-1], so need S[i] <= C[t-1].
        // Thus valid t are [1..M] with t-1 >= lower_bound(C, S[i]).
        int firstFit = lower_bound(C.begin(), C.end(), S[i]) - C.begin() + 1; // 1-based t
        if (firstFit > M) continue;

        for (int t = M; t >= firstFit; --t) {
            if (dp[t - 1] <= V[i]) {
                dp[t] = min(dp[t], V[i]);
                ans = max(ans, t);
            }
        }
    }

    cout << ans << '\n';
    return 0;
}

